{"name":"Cold Feet","hex":"#bed4fd","fetched":"2026-10-23","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cold feet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of nervousness or anxiety causing one to reconsider a decision about an upcoming event.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see cold,‎ feet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cold_feet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cold Feet","blocks":[{"text":"Cold Feet is a British comedy drama television series produced by Granada Television for the ITV network. The series was created and principally written by Mike Bullen as a follow-up to his 1997 Comedy Premiere special of the same name. The series follows three couples experiencing the ups-and-downs of romance, originally Adam Williams and Rachel Bradley, Pete and Jenny Gifford and Karen and David Marsden. As the original series progressed, the Giffords divorced and Pete married Jo Ellison, whilst Karen and David also separated, forming relationships with Mark Cubitt and Robyn Duff."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_Feet","wikidata":"Q2225255"},{"title":"Cold feet","blocks":[{"text":"Cold feet is a phrase that refers to a person not going through with an action, particularly one which requires long-term commitment, due to fear, uncertainty, and doubt. A person is said to be \"getting cold feet\" when, after previously committing to a plan, they ultimately do not carry out the planned course of action."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_feet","wikidata":"Q5142448"},{"title":"Cold","blocks":[{"text":"Cold is the presence of low temperature, especially in the atmosphere. In common usage, cold is often a subjective perception. A lower bound to temperature is absolute zero, defined as 0.00 K on the Kelvin scale, an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. This corresponds to −273.15 °C on the Celsius scale, −459.67 °F on the Fahrenheit scale, and 0.00 °R on the Rankine scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/330px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold","wikidata":"Q270952"},{"title":"Foot","blocks":[{"text":"The foot is an anatomical structure found in many vertebrates. It is the terminal portion of a limb which bears weight and allows locomotion. In many animals with feet, the foot is an organ at the terminal part of the leg made up of one or more segments or bones, generally including claws and/or nails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Foot_on_white_background.jpg/330px-Foot_on_white_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foot","wikidata":"Q15807"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/James_Nesbitt_July_2008.jpg/500px-James_Nesbitt_July_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/James_Nesbitt_July_2008.jpg/500px-James_Nesbitt_July_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJames_Nesbitt_July_2008.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Helen_Baxendale.jpg/250px-Helen_Baxendale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Helen_Baxendale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Helen_Baxendale.jpg/250px-Helen_Baxendale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHelen_Baxendale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3e/Cold_Feet_cast_regular.jpg/330px-Cold_Feet_cast_regular.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3e/Cold_Feet_cast_regular.jpg/330px-Cold_Feet_cast_regular.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"(from left): Robert Bathurst, Hermione Norris, James Nesbitt, Helen Baxendale, John Thomson, and Fay Ripley","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACold_Feet_cast_regular.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Tom_Hooper_-_Flickr_-_Eva_Rinaldi_Celebrity_and_Live_Music_Photographer_%281%29.jpg/500px-Tom_Hooper_-_Flickr_-_Eva_Rinaldi_Celebrity_and_Live_Music_Photographer_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Tom_Hooper_-_Flickr_-_Eva_Rinaldi_Celebrity_and_Live_Music_Photographer_%281%29.jpg/500px-Tom_Hooper_-_Flickr_-_Eva_Rinaldi_Celebrity_and_Live_Music_Photographer_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cold Feet had a number of directors, including Tom Hooper, who directed two episodes of the second series in 1999","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATom_Hooper_-_Flickr_-_Eva_Rinaldi_Celebrity_and_Live_Music_Photographer_(1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Bridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg/500px-Bridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Bridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg/1280px-Bridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Bridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg/500px-Bridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cold Feet was filmed on location around Manchester, such as at Bridgewater Hall in the city centre","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABridgewater_Hall_in_2008.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/500px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/500px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An iceberg, which is commonly associated with cold","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Signal \"cold\" – unofficial (except recommended by CMAS), it is nonetheless used by many schools of diving and propagated through diving websites as one of the more useful additional signals[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiving_signal_cold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Goose bumps, a common physiological response to cold, aiming to reduce the loss of body heat in a cold environment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoose_bumps.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Cold feet in Priddy - geograph.org.uk - 5711642","ima